        <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl lg:text-4xl text-ink leading-tight mb-5">What is ADHD? Recognising Attention Deficit Symptoms in Children</h1>
        <p class="text-ink-muted text-lg leading-relaxed">Your child can't sit still in class, leaves homework half-finished, or forgets instructions the moment you give them. Is this just high energy — or a sign they need support? Recognising ADHD is the first step toward getting it right.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-12 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <div class="prose-custom space-y-8">

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What is ADHD?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental condition that affects a child's attention span, impulse control and activity level. It stems from differences in how the brain's executive functions develop — it is not the result of laziness, poor parenting or inadequate discipline.</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Symptoms typically begin before the age of 12 and are observed in at least two settings — for example, both at home and at school. Many children receive a diagnosis during primary school years, although some are not identified until adolescence.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-primary/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">DSM-5 diagnostic criterion: the six-month rule</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">An ADHD diagnosis requires that at least six symptoms of inattention or hyperactivity-impulsivity have persisted for at least six months, are inconsistent with the child's developmental level, and are impairing functioning across more than one setting.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What do ADHD symptoms look like in children?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">ADHD symptoms fall into three main groups. Not every child will show all three — which is part of what makes identification challenging:</p>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-4 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <p class="text-xs font-medium text-ink mb-2">Inattention</p>
                <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                  <li>• Difficulty noticing details</li>
                  <li>• Unable to sustain attention on tasks or play</li>
                  <li>• Seems not to listen when spoken to directly</li>
                  <li>• Fails to follow through on instructions</li>
                  <li>• Frequently loses belongings</li>
                  <li>• Easily distracted by external stimuli</li>
                  <li>• Forgetful in daily activities</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-4 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <p class="text-xs font-medium text-ink mb-2">Hyperactivity</p>
                <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                  <li>• Fidgets with hands or feet</li>
                  <li>• Leaves seat when expected to remain seated</li>
                  <li>• Runs or climbs in inappropriate situations</li>
                  <li>• Difficulty playing quietly</li>
                  <li>• Often "on the go" or driven by a motor</li>
                  <li>• Talks excessively</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-4 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <p class="text-xs font-medium text-ink mb-2">Impulsivity</p>
                <ul class="text-xs text-ink-muted space-y-1.5">
                  <li>• Blurts out answers before a question is finished</li>
                  <li>• Can't wait their turn</li>
                  <li>• Interrupts or intrudes on others</li>
                  <li>• Acts without thinking about consequences</li>
                  <li>• Extreme emotional reactions</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What parents and teachers typically notice</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">ADHD presents differently depending on the setting. Here is what is most commonly reported:</p>
            <div class="space-y-3">
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">At home</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Getting up repeatedly while doing homework, being able to sit at a screen for hours but unable to hold a book for five minutes, constantly losing things, difficulty remembering household rules, sudden conflicts with siblings.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">At school</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Struggling to follow lessons, copying errors, inability to remain seated, difficulty waiting their turn, friction in group work, poor use of exam time.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">In social situations</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Disregarding or quickly breaking the rules of a game, fragile friendships, sudden outbursts of anger, very low tolerance for frustration.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-primary/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">A common misconception: "But they can play video games for hours"</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Children with ADHD can focus intensely on activities they find highly engaging — this is called hyperfocus. It does not rule out ADHD; rather, it reflects how their brain's reward system works. The core difficulty is not an absence of attention but an inability to direct attention voluntarily.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Types of ADHD</h2>
            <div class="space-y-3">
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Predominantly Inattentive Type</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Hyperactivity is not prominent; the child may appear calm or even dreamy. More commonly seen in girls, which means diagnosis is often delayed.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive Type</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Motor restlessness and impulse control are more prominent than attention difficulties. More frequently seen in boys and more likely to be identified early.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Combined Type</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Both symptom groups are present and significant. This is the most common presentation of ADHD.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">How to tell ADHD apart from typical child development</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Not every energetic child has ADHD. These questions can help you distinguish:</p>
            <ul class="space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Are the symptoms visible in <strong class="text-ink">more than one setting</strong> — both at home and at school?</p>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Are the behaviours negatively affecting the child's <strong class="text-ink">academic performance, friendships or self-esteem</strong>?</p>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Has this been going on <strong class="text-ink">consistently for at least six months</strong>?</p>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Are these behaviours <strong class="text-ink">noticeably more intense than in peers</strong> of the same age?</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">If you answer "yes" to most of these, it is worth speaking with a specialist.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">How does the assessment process work?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">An ADHD diagnosis is not based on a single test. A thorough evaluation typically involves:</p>
            <ul class="space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <div class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs font-semibold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Clinical interviews</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Separate interviews with the child and parents covering developmental history, school performance and family dynamics.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <div class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs font-semibold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Parent and teacher rating scales</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Standardised tools such as the Conners or Vanderbilt scales measure symptom severity across different settings.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <div class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs font-semibold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Psychological testing</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Tests measuring attention, memory and executive functions help distinguish ADHD from learning difficulties, anxiety or other conditions.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <div class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs font-semibold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5">4</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Medical evaluation</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Medical causes of similar symptoms — such as hearing difficulties, thyroid problems or sleep disorders — are ruled out.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Treatment and support options</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">ADHD is a treatable condition. The most effective approach combines more than one strategy:</p>
            <ul class="space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Behavioural Therapy</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">The first-line choice, particularly for children under 6. It teaches the child self-regulation skills and helps parents build consistent structure at home.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Parent Training</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Expert guidance on communication strategies, reward systems and home routines. Research shows that parent training is as effective as direct child therapy for many families.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT)</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Particularly helpful for school-age children and adolescents. Addresses organisation, time management and unhelpful thinking patterns.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">School Accommodations</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Extended time in exams, a front-row seat, homework tracking tools — reasonable adjustments that can significantly improve academic outcomes.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-primary shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Medication</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-0.5">Prescribed by a psychiatrist after full evaluation. For moderate to severe presentations, stimulant or non-stimulant medication combined with behavioural support can produce meaningful results.</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-accent/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-accent/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">Why early support matters</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Children who grow up without a diagnosis are sometimes labelled "lazy", "disrespectful" or "a failure". These labels erode self-confidence and increase the risk of secondary anxiety or depression. Early intervention can break that cycle and bring the child's strengths to the fore.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
